@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# Local native ACP completion patch
+
+## Recipe-bound tool evidence and narrow direct-edit qualification
+
+The response JSON schema annotation `x-goose-tool-evidence` accepts `required` or `not_required`. Omission defaults to required; malformed values are rejected. Native completion now distinguishes no_tools_observed from explicit unobservable activity. Only an explicit not_required contract may accept no_tools_observed, with the same strict final JSON, schema, terminal, typed outcome and no-failed/no-pending requirements. Unsupported/unobservable activity is rejected under both policies. No-tools-observed is an observation statement, not proof that invisible activity never occurred or that external effects were verified.
+
+The policy is part of the recipe already hashed into the receipt binding, now native-completion/3. A policy-only change invalidates a saved receipt. Legacy missing observation fields remain unobservable; historical receipts are not silently upgraded. Writer/verifier recipes explicitly require evidence, while the analysis-only example does not attest tool effects.
+
+The actual pinned codex-acp handler and renderer generate 14 fixture sequences. An in-memory ACP agent transports those exact rendered updates to the real Goose provider, then the real native completion validator checks both policies (28 acceptance decisions). Cases include direct edit success/failure, duplicate starts and status-only terminal patches, contradictory/reordered/missing events, opaque/unsupported activity (also mixed with successful edits), legitimate analysis without events, malformed JSON and missing final output. Identical empty terminal patches no longer conflict with previously accumulated start content; contradictory outcomes still fail.
+
+This qualifies event mapping and acceptance, not enforcement of the model's native tool roster. Installed CLI feature-disable candidates were inspected locally with network and credentials denied. They accept the flags and disable code-mode host/shell features, but unified_exec remains enabled in the feature tables; feature state does not itself prove exposure. No authoritative direct-edit-only builtin roster has been established. The future live driver blocks on the failed roster qualification before setup or launch. No live run is permitted by this offline work.
+
+
+## Offline boundary and observation correction
+
+This follow-up preserves ACP messageId and generic local-guard message phase metadata. The final candidate is the last distinct message, not all text since the last tool event. Chunking within that message is preserved; interleaved identities or disappearance of an established identity are rejected. Strict whole-document JSON validation remains. Commentary is not treated as final output.
+
+Native terminal evidence and receipts now record final_message_id, final_message_phase and tool_observation (observed_success, observed_failure, unobservable). Completion requires observed_success and no failed/pending calls. Zero observed tool calls, unsupported opaque results and unsettled calls cannot attest successful work. This intentionally does not qualify text-only native tasks with no observed tools. The existing boolean tool_error means observed protocol error, not proof that all native execution was visible.
+
+The binding includes native-completion/2, so earlier receipts remain historical evidence and cannot be silently reused under the stricter contract. No historical session database is rewritten. Existing tool-capable API providers and real final_output behavior are unchanged.
+
+The paired POC supplies a hash/version-pinned isolated codex-acp 2.2.2 guard. It forwards message phase without advertising proprietary client capabilities; opaque functionCallOutput or unsupported item kinds are reported as unobservable. Such a result fails the evidence contract; it is not a claim that execution itself failed. Both installed Codex 0.160.1 and 0.163.0 schemas include this opaque type, and neither gives it typed execution success. The guard does not parse prose to invent status and cannot qualify successful nested-exec work. Normalized offline replay demonstrates the adapter's drop, but original app-server wire traffic was not captured.
+
+All verification in this follow-up is offline. The broad model-driven goose-self-test recipe remains outside the authorization; its inventory was updated. A new live proof, global replacement, upstream submission or expanded tool permissions requires separate scope.
+
+
+This is an isolated experimental change on official Goose v1.55.0 (`2bcb8583a18a0623eb78c9ed845d597ed99fae48`), related to upstream issue #10275. No public submission, global binary replacement, production rollout or broader Flow migration is part of it.
+
+Enable only for qualified disposable runs with `GOOSE_NATIVE_ACP_COMPLETION=true`. The Codex and Claude ACP factories opt into the new native-completion capability; other ACP factories remain off. Unset the variable to retain the previous structured-recipe rejection. Standard tool-capable providers continue to use real `final_output` calls and the same schema validator.
+
+The patch carries typed native terminal evidence through an additive provider stream. It uses the ACP terminal stop reason, final text after the last tool activity, and a tool ledger; EOF, refusal, truncation, failure, unresolved tools or invalid JSON do not imply success. Strict native parsing rejects duplicate keys, mixed prose/documents, precision-changing number normalization, excessive size/depth and external schema references. No model-based repair or fake tool acknowledgement is used.
+
+The existing response schema is preserved. The delegated recipe instructions and schema are supplied in actual native prompt content, since ACP ignores Goose's system/tool channels. Validated reports are persisted as versioned receipts bound to session, task/recipe, model/provider, working directory, mode and extension selection. Existing foreground subagent result delivery consumes receipts. An interrupted attempt without a receipt is blocked for reconciliation instead of being replayed automatically. Cancelled completion commit cannot be restored as success.
+
+Cancellation on dropped native stream sends `session/cancel` while the prompt is active, then allows a bounded drain. Failure to drain stops the connection; side effects remain uncertain. Notification/terminal sends use bounded backpressure. SDK notification handlers run in receive order; the transport saturation fixture checks the completion ordering. Stream termination does not prove arbitrary subprocess quiescence or roll back file effects; the disposable proof independently observes its own process scope.
+
+## Verification
+
+Use the repository-pinned Rust 1.99.0 toolchain and locked Cargo dependencies. Goose's unit test suite requires `scheduler,tree-sitter` even for focused filters. Native and regression tests use fake providers/ACP protocol fixtures, not Ollama or authenticated native inference. See the task-level regression logs and result manifest for actual outcomes; do not infer a pass from this document.
+
+The live qualification, only after offline checks, is the separately authorized 8-parent-turn / 4-child-Goose-turn / 10-minute create → update → different-native-agent Read verification fixture. It uses existing subscription connections and applies each native worker sandbox once. The host seeds the sentinel only; it does not perform the target-file operations. Native inner model requests are distinct from Goose turns and must not be mislabeled as independently capped by the Goose counter.
+
+The general goose-self-test recipe's feature inventory is updated, but running that broad model-driven suite would exceed the authorized live budget. Use the focused offline fixtures plus the bounded approved live proof instead.
+
+## Upstream and upkeep
+
+The provider stream API is additive but touches published GDK crate surfaces. Review that API, native request preparation, cancellation/transport ordering, receipt persistence and foreground delivery together. A small schema-deletion-only patch is insufficient. Draft upstream PR #12778 may change these seams; do not merge or duplicate its backend redesign without review.
+
+Upstream issue status/design must be reconciled before any public PR. Repository AGENTS.md permits this local exploratory implementation without a Ready issue. No issue/PR posting is authorized.
+
+Rollback: unset the opt-in, or return to the unchanged stock executable. Keep the isolated patch checkout and receipt/evidence history. Do not overwrite a live executable. Old in-flight/receipt records remain audit data and require reconciliation if their bound contract changes; rollback must not redispatch work.
+
+Not solved: transparent native context recovery after host death, exactly-once external file effects, arbitrary daemon tracking, general CLI-provider structured completion, broad system-prompt parity, automatic repair, or production hardening. The initial slice deliberately fails on a reported tool error even if the native agent later claims to have repaired it.
+
+## Verified local checkpoint
+
+Current-source checks passed: 113 ACP-provider tests; 128 state-machine tests; seven builtin-output tests; focused strict-validation/receipt tests; four protocol transport fixtures; 17 shared inference/cancellation/tool tests; and 631 provider-type tests. The ACP bootstrap and subagent-runner regressions passed as well. Counts overlap between focused filters and broader groups; they are not additive totals. Formatting, strict Clippy across the selected crates/all targets, and the isolated CLI build passed. All-target lint includes `acp-http` because upstream test imports require it. No local-inference feature or real provider invocation was used by these checks.
+
+The earlier parallel ACP run was interrupted after one existing shared-config handoff test hung. That test passed in isolation, then all ACP tests passed serially. This is recorded as an interrupted run followed by successful serial checks, not a clean initial parallel pass.
